riscv: esp32s31: default the PIE/HWLoop switch hook off

The hook makes an SBI ecall into M-mode on EVERY context switch to save 216
bytes of PIE state plus the HWLoop CSRs for the outgoing task and restore them
for the incoming one. Unlike __switch_to_fpu() it is unconditional, because
there is no hardware dirty bit to test.

Measured with rootfs/switchbench.c arm C, three runs per arm, first arm
repeated as a control:

    hook on    150.35 157.60 156.63 us
    hook off   105.40 102.10 104.86 us
    hook on    158.65 154.18 153.98 us   (control reproduces)

50.8 us of a 155 us switch - a third of it.

WHY THIS IS SAFE HERE, and it is a build-time invariant, not a runtime one:

    Makefile: S31_USER_ISA = rv32imafbc_zicsr_zifencei_zaamo_zalrsc_zba_zbb_
                             zbc_zbs

No xespv (PIE) and no xesploop. The kernel's own ISA has neither either -
/proc/cpuinfo reports rv32imafc_zicntr_zicsr_zifencei_zaamo_zalrsc_zca_zcf_
zba_zbb_zbc_zbs. No compiler on this system emits a coprocessor instruction,
so no task can dirty the state this was preserving. Note the comment above
S31_USER_ISA claims userspace "keeps PIE"; the ISA string it actually sets
does not, and the string is what the compiler obeys.

IF PIE OR HWLOOP IS EVER PUT BACK IN S31_USER_ISA, SET THIS BACK TO true.
Nothing detects the mistake: the failure is one task reading another's vector
registers, silently and rarely.

Verified after the change: md5sum of the same file is identical 6/6 and again
under concurrent load (that is the test that caught the earlier xesploop
corruption), audio plays, bluetoothd/s31-bt/lvdesk all run, an X client
launches through the shim, and input lag is unchanged.

Regenerate with:

    diff -u linux-esp32-s31/arch/riscv/kernel/esp32s31-ext.c \
            linux-71-port/arch/riscv/kernel/esp32s31-ext.c \
            --label a/arch/riscv/kernel/esp32s31-ext.c \
            --label b/arch/riscv/kernel/esp32s31-ext.c

--- a/arch/riscv/kernel/esp32s31-ext.c
+++ b/arch/riscv/kernel/esp32s31-ext.c
@@ -37,27 +37,50 @@
 }
 
 /*
- * A context switch on this board costs ~517 us, against 2-5 us on comparable
- * hardware, and that dominates everything: SD requests spend 8.3 switches each,
- * and USB wakes a kworker on every one of its ~1000 interrupts a second.
- *
- * This hook is the prime suspect, because unlike __switch_to_fpu() just above
- * it in switch_to.h it is completely unconditional - every switch makes an SBI
- * ecall into M-mode to save 216 bytes of PIE state plus the HWLoop CSRs for the
- * outgoing task and restore them for the incoming one, whether or not either
- * task has ever touched the coprocessor.
- *
- * Count it, and allow it to be skipped, so the cost can be attributed before
- * anything is redesigned:
- *
- *     /sys/kernel/debug/esp32s31_ext/stats
- *     echo 0 > /sys/module/kernel/parameters/esp32s31_ext_switch_enabled
- *
- * Skipping is a MEASUREMENT AID ONLY and is unsafe: any task actually using PIE
- * or hardware loops will see another task's coprocessor state. Defaults on, and
- * a reset restores it.
+ * Save and restore the PIE/HWLoop coprocessor across a context switch -
+ * DEFAULT OFF, because on this system nothing can have any state to save.
+ *
+ * The hook is unconditional by nature: unlike __switch_to_fpu() just above it
+ * in switch_to.h there is no hardware dirty bit to test, so every switch made
+ * an SBI ecall into M-mode to save 216 bytes of PIE state plus the HWLoop CSRs
+ * for the outgoing task and restore them for the incoming one, whether or not
+ * either had ever touched the coprocessor.
+ *
+ * Measured with rootfs/switchbench.c arm C (sched_yield between two runnable
+ * tasks, which isolates the switch), three runs per arm, first arm repeated:
+ *
+ *     hook on    150.35 157.60 156.63 us
+ *     hook off   105.40 102.10 104.86 us
+ *     hook on    158.65 154.18 153.98 us   (control reproduces)
+ *
+ * 50.8 us of a 155 us switch: a THIRD of the cost, on the most frequent
+ * operation on a one-core machine.
+ *
+ * THE INVARIANT THAT MAKES THIS SAFE, and it is a build-time one:
+ *
+ *   Makefile: S31_USER_ISA = rv32imafbc_zicsr_zifencei_zaamo_zalrsc_zba_zbb_
+ *                            zbc_zbs
+ *
+ * There is no xespv (PIE) and no xesploop in it, and none in the kernel's own
+ * ISA either - /proc/cpuinfo reports rv32imafc_zicntr_zicsr_zifencei_zaamo_
+ * zalrsc_zca_zcf_zba_zbb_zbc_zbs. No compiler on this system emits a
+ * coprocessor instruction, so no task can dirty the state this was preserving.
+ * The comment in the Makefile above S31_USER_ISA says userspace "keeps PIE";
+ * the ISA string it actually sets does not, and the string is the truth.
+ *
+ * xesploop is separately and permanently banned: the loop CSRs are M-mode
+ * only, hart0 saves coprocessor state per TRAP rather than per task, and a
+ * long loop could return with someone else's counter - which is how md5sum
+ * came to report false SD corruption.
+ *
+ * IF YOU RE-ENABLE PIE OR HWLOOP IN S31_USER_ISA, SET THIS BACK TO true.
+ * Nothing detects the mistake for you: the failure is one task reading
+ * another's vector registers, silently and rarely.
+ *
+ *     echo 1 > /sys/module/kernel/parameters/esp32s31_ext_switch_enabled
+ *     /sys/kernel/debug/esp32s31_ext/stats     (DIAG=1 builds only)
  */
-static bool s31_ext_switch_enabled = true;
+static bool s31_ext_switch_enabled;
 core_param(esp32s31_ext_switch_enabled, s31_ext_switch_enabled, bool, 0644);
 
 static bool s31_ext_profile;
